    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Soul Manipulation was on the stack targeting Whirler Rogue (Gem25F Analyst's own spell). The player had the option to counter their own Whirler Rogue with mode 1, but that makes no sense since it's their own creature. However, looking more carefully, Soul Manipulation has two modes: counter a creature spell AND/OR return a creature card from graveyard to hand. The player chose Cancel (selecting 0 modes effectively, or declining the counter mode). But Soul Manipulation requires choosing at least 1 mode (min 1). The player had Notorious Throng, Murder, and Myriad Landscape in their graveyard - none are creature cards. So the second mode (return creature from graveyard) has no valid targets. The first mode would counter their own Whirler Rogue, which is terrible. So Cancel here seems like it might be the interface's way of declining the counter mode, and the spell resolves doing nothing useful. But wait - why did they cast Soul Manipulation in the first place if neither mode is beneficial? They're countering nothing useful and have no creatures in graveyard. This appears to be a waste of the card Soul Manipulation entirely. Actually, re-reading: the Soul Manipulation is already on the stack. The decision is about choosing modes. If they must choose at least 1 mode, and the only viable mode is countering their own creature (bad), then Cancel might be correct to avoid that. But the real blunder may have been casting Soul Manipulation in the first place with no good targets. However, we're only evaluating this specific decision. Choosing Cancel (not countering their own Whirler Rogue) is clearly correct here - letting Whirler Rogue resolve is much better than countering your own creature.",
